    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">How a Reverse Mortgage Actually Works: A Plain-Language Guide for Homeowners 62 and Older</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you're 62 or older, own your home, and have wondered whether a reverse mortgage might make sense for your situation but never fully understood how it works, this article is written for you. The concept gets discussed in broad strokes far more often than it gets explained clearly, and most homeowners deserve better than a vague summary before making a decision this significant.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Basic Mechanic Nobody Explains Simply Enough</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A reverse mortgage lets you convert a portion of your home equity into cash without selling your home or making monthly mortgage payments. Instead of you paying the lender each month, the lender pays you, or makes funds available to you, and the loan balance grows over time. The loan is repaid when you sell the home, move out permanently, or pass away. You remain the owner of the home throughout. That's the core of it, and most of the confusion about reverse mortgages comes from not having that baseline clearly established first.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Part That Surprises Most People: You Still Own the Home</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">One of the most persistent misconceptions about reverse mortgages is that the bank takes ownership of your home. It does not. You retain the title. You remain responsible for property taxes, homeowner's insurance, and basic maintenance, and as long as those obligations are met and the home remains your primary residence, you cannot be forced out.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The lender holds a lien against the property, the same as with any mortgage. The difference is that instead of the balance going down over time as you make payments, it goes up as interest accrues. The loan comes due when the home is sold or is no longer your primary residence.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">This structure is the source of both the product's value and its risks, and understanding it clearly is the necessary starting point for any decision about whether it fits your situation.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How the Money Actually Comes to You</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The most common reverse mortgage product is the Home Equity Conversion Mortgage, or HECM, which is insured by the Federal Housing Administration. HECMs offer several ways to receive funds, and the right structure depends on what you need the money to accomplish.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A lump sum provides a single disbursement at closing, typically at a fixed rate. A line of credit makes funds available to draw as needed, with the unused portion growing over time at a rate tied to interest. Monthly payments provide a steady income stream for a set term or for as long as you remain in the home. Most borrowers can combine these options to match their specific cash flow needs.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The amount you can access depends on your age, your home's appraised value, current interest rates, and the FHA lending limit. Generally, the older you are and the more equity you hold, the more you can access.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Confirm you meet the basic eligibility requirements.</strong> You must be 62 or older, own your home outright or have substantial equity, occupy the home as your primary residence, and be current on property taxes and insurance.</li>
      <li><strong>Complete the required HUD counseling session before you apply.</strong> Federal law requires all HECM borrowers to meet with an independent HUD-approved counselor before proceeding. This session is genuinely useful, not just a formality. It covers your obligations, the loan structure, and alternatives worth considering.</li>
      <li><strong>Get a clear picture of your current equity position.</strong> Your available equity is the foundation of what a reverse mortgage can offer you. Know that number before you start evaluating options.</li>
      <li><strong>Think through how you want to receive the funds.</strong> Lump sum, line of credit, monthly payments, or a combination. The right structure depends on whether you need immediate cash, supplemental income, or a financial safety net.</li>
      <li><strong>Involve family members in the conversation early if they're part of your life planning.</strong> Reverse mortgages affect how your home factors into your estate. Having that conversation before you apply is better than having it after.</li>
    </ol>

